Assignment 24: Write a R program to create a factor corresponding to height of women data set, which
contains height and weights for a sample of women
Solution: # Load the built-in 'women' dataset (it is usually available by default, but explicitly calling data()
ensures availability)
data(women)
# Display the first few rows of the original data for reference
print("Original 'women' data set (first 6 rows):")
print(head(women))
# Use the cut() function to create a factor for height.
# Option 1: Divide into a specific number of equal-length intervals (e.g., 3)
height_factor_equal <- cut(women$height, 3)
print("\nFactor corresponding to height (3 equal intervals):")
print(table(height_factor_equal)) # Display the frequency table
# Option 2: Divide into user-defined intervals with custom labels (e.g., Short, Medium, Tall)
# Define the break points (minimum height is 58, maximum is 72 in this dataset)
breaks <- c(58, 63, 67, 72)
labels <- c("Short", "Medium", "Tall")
height_factor_custom <- cut(women$height, breaks = breaks, labels = labels, include.lowest = TRUE)
print("\nFactor corresponding to height (custom labels and breaks):")
print(table(height_factor_custom)) # Display the frequency table
# Add the new factor variable to the 'women' data frame (optional)
women$height_group <- height_factor_custom
print("\n'women' data set with new height_group factor (first 6 rows):")
print(head(women))



